INT: What did your family do for a living?

RG: My father had a flour mill, which he had just outside the city. And we were fairly well off, but they robbed us of everything. Luckily, we had a neighbor across the street who was very fond of my grandmother, my paternal grandmother, because he was her Shabbos goy at the time. And he came over and he said, come and I'll hide you, because there were bands going around killing Jews. Well, he did hide us in his barn. And luckily, they didn't find us. And-- but they went into the house, because they knew it was a Jewish house. It was full of, you know, things that belonged to my grandmother before. She had eight kids. There was everything in that house. And my mother, through a small, like a little aperture in the wall, saw them carrying out things a whole day. They were just robbing us. And when we were-- it quieted down, and we were able to go back to the house, it was totally empty. And--

INT: Do you remember anything that they took, what kind of things?

RG: Well, anything. They took furniture, clothing. Whatever you have in the house, they took. They have not-- they-- Jews in-- had things. They had nothing. So whatever they saw that they needed or wanted, they-- it was for the taking. Was OK. [LAUGHING] And after that, as I said, we went to the ghetto. And then we lived in fear all the time. And that fear remained in me. I didn't understand what was happening, but the fear-- I lived-- Because in an atmosphere of total of constant fear, I became very fearful. And I remember that, for years, my mother had so stay with me. I wouldn't go to sleep unless the light was on in the room. Because I probably thought that in the dark, you get taken away or something like that. And that's how we managed to live. But we were lucky than most of our family that did go to Transnistria. Mostly, they were children my age, younger or older. And they didn't come back. And a lot of the adults died from-- just from starvation and from sickness, from-- there were no medicine. We had-- my parents had a friend, a doctor. And he said, I'm so glad that I was a doctor. You know why? Because he could steal a piece of soap. And with that, he saved his family. Because people did not have any-- not enough water, let alone soap. They had no changes in clothing. You know, unless they washed what they wore, they didn't have. So they died from this misery. And starvation was terrible, terrible. They tried to set up, I think, a kitchen like for these-- the people outside, I guess, in-- who were in other countries. In the United States, they started to send through organizations things to save us. But a lot of it didn't get there. Or maybe-- I don't know. Maybe it was stolen. But anyways, there was a kitchen, like for the inmates there, but the food didn't get to them. And it was very, very sad-- very sad. From time to time, we used to get that this one died and that one died, and it was constant crying and misery.

INT: How were you getting the information?

RG: I think that maybe sometimes they could send a letter through the Red Cross, or maybe sometimes officers would-- like, somebody would approach an officer or somebody. Could you tell my family who lives at so and so and so that so and so died? I imagine that. I'm not sure how they got the news, actually. But it filtered through.

INT: Mhm.

RG: So-- and that's-- there are many stories. That's how we were lucky we survived.

INT: What did you do for food?

RG: For food? Well, there used to be-- the peasants used to have a market. They had a market. So my mother-- sometimes the way my mother made a living was that she did not look-- if you see, she-- I don't know if you see in these pictures. She did not look like a typical Jew. She was blonde. She was-- they thought she was a shiksa, and she hid her yellow star to hide it and hope that she would escape. My mother also made some-- she was very young. She had no business experience, but she was clever. And she sold things that-- you know, Jews needed money there. You cannot eat furniture or whatever else. So my mother would-- they would give her certain things, certain household goods and so. And then she would approach the Gentiles and sell it for probably a fraction of what it was worth. But she-- they gave her a certain amount of money. So with that, we could live. It was very difficult. But most of the food you got, the peasants would bring things into the city. And they would go maybe from house to house and sell this and sell that. So you needed some money to subside on that too. Yeah, it's-- and, yes, there are many, many stories there. My grandmother was living with us because my grandfather was just shot on the street. He also tried to hide. My grandmother came to visit us. And because she wanted to help my mother with me. And my grandfather was also in hiding. He got-- and as he was walking home, when he found out that it was safe to walk home, a patrol of soldiers came, and he was shot. So at that point, my grandmother had no home anymore, and she stayed with us. And she looked after me most, and I became very close to her because she looked after me. And my mother was always trying to make a few dollars or money, a little bit of money to be able to sustain us. So--

INT: Where was your father?

RG: My father, that's another story. My father was taken on forced labor for three years.

INT: When?

RG: At the beginning of the war. He and most of the other young Jewish men were taken on forced labor. It was very difficult because he was-- they were building roads. And the men that he-- the young men that he was with were not used to physical labor. A lot of them didn't know how to hold a shovel, how to-- and they had a quota to do. And it was very dangerous, and a lot of them dropped. They couldn't keep it to do the quota. My father was lucky because he was physically fit. He always worked outdoors. And he was able to finish the quota. As a matter of fact, he used to help some of them. And he-- also, peasants used to come to the-- at night, used to come because he had a little money, so he could buy whatever they sold, whatever-- cheese, whatever they sold. He got a little extra to eat because the portions there and the quality of the food was horrible. And he was there for three years. He used to come-- he came home for a few weeks, and then they would call him back again. And this happened three times. The third time was the war was already over, and it was a terrible confusion. And he was able to run away to Bucharest, which was the capital of Romania. And eventually, word got to my mother that he escaped and went-- and we were liberated by the Russians. And for a small time, we could go. We could move into Romania because that was already under Russian rule. So we met him after the war there and reunited. But my mother always wanted to come back-- come to Canada because her family, three brothers-- and as it is, my grandmother had to go a year earlier before we were-- we went out of Romania. So-- she-- Sorry. So she met-- no, I forgot what I was going to say. Just a minute. Can you stop for a second?

