RG: Just keep going? OK. OK. My-- so my mother always wanted-- when she said if I lived the war, I have to go back to-- I have to go to Canada because I have my whole family there. But it took us years. She kept on making application, and we were rejected. Finally, my mother-- my grandmother got permission. And much to my mother's anxieties, she had to travel by herself to Canada and to-- came here-- came to Canada a year before us. And the meantime, Ben-Gurion made a pact with the Romanians to send out the Jews. I did not know until recently that he had-- that Israel had to pay for every one of us that came out. And we came to Israel. And as soon as we came to Israel-- it was right after. It was in 1950. So it was right after the War of Independence. And it was very difficult in Israel. It was much confusion, as you know, so many different people from so many different countries and from so many different ways of life. And so we finally made an application to go to Canada, and we were approved. And the rest is history. We came to Canada. We were very lucky. We-- my-- our family was a very close family, and they were very, very happy to have us and helped us a lot. And we-- thank God, we made a life here.

INT: Do you remember what life was like in-- at home, back in Europe when the Russians took over?

RG: When the Russians took over? That-- it was very-- it was not as bad as the Germans, but it was a terrible regime. Communism is a terrible regime. And--

INT: Do you remember, did you go to school? Did you work?

RG: I went to school. Oh, yes, I definitely went to school. I went up to grade six. And my father had a very-- even though you were not allowed to have private properties or private businesses, he had a very small mill in the town, in the small town we lived. And he eked out some kind of a living. But we lived also in constant fear because if you-- you didn't know who to trust. Your best friend could turn against you, and you could be-- they said you had whatever, make up a story, and they put you right in jail. And to go into jail there was like writing your life away. And-- But finally, we-- he-- as I said, we were able to go to Israel, and my parents were very happy in Israel. They had lots of friends. They had-- but life was very difficult. And then my mother was-- now that she had her mother there and her three brothers, she was very anxious to come here. And--

INT: Where-- how did you get to Canada?

RG: How did-- well, we got to Canada. We went first to Italy. And from Italy, we took a boat to Canada. And my uncles came to see us. We were able to come to right to Halifax. And it was--

INT: Do you remember that boat ride?

RG: The boat ride was OK. We got a little sick, but that was nothing. Because we knew that, at the end, we were going to go to a country that, you know, we were welcomed by family. We-- life was good. It was freedom.

INT: But you didn't speak English.

RG: I spoke very little because I started to take lessons in-- when we were in Israel. My mother wanted to prepare me to go back to school. So we had-- I had a private teacher who came to the-- yeah. And she taught me. I knew some, but I learned quickly because I was young. I wasn't-- made friends and so forth. And at first, it was very difficult in Canada. My parents worked very hard, and it was a difficult adjustment. But they had friends here, and they had, of course, the family here, who were very supportive. And then in 1958, I met Lony actually before 1958, but we got married in 1958.

INT: Where--

RG: And--

INT: What was life like in Canada-- in Toronto then?

RG: In Toronto was--

INT: Did you go to Jewish schools? Did you go to synagogues? Did you celebrate?

RG: Yes, in Canada, it was good because we had freedom. We had-- my parents didn't have much, but whatever they had, they lived. They had friends, my family. It was a normal life. You did not have to live in fear. And to us, it looked like, you know, even though, like Toronto looked to us-- it was not a like a cultured and so city, but we had freedom. You could walk the street. You could-- my parents were so happy when they heard of a Jew being in the government. We never had that. Schools were open to everyone. Even in the best of times in Europe, they had what-- they had a quota. But the quota was not a quota. No, no. Like medical school or any profession you wanted to study, there was a restriction how many Jews they took into that. So it it was a good life here, and as it is now, too. We consider it to be one of the best in the world. So it was OK.

INT: Were you met with any antisemitism or racism here?

